But `native-runtime.ts` uses `process.resourcesPath`, typed? In Electron main, `process.resourcesPath` is an Electron extension. `@types/node` process may not have it; Electron's types augment NodeJS.Process with `resourcesPath`? Electron's `electron.d.ts` declares `process.resourcesPath`. Since tsconfig types node only but electron package types included via import elsewhere? In this module we don't import electron, so TypeScript may not know `resourcesPath`. Use `(process as NodeJS.Process & { resourcesPath?: string }).resourcesPath`. Or import { app } from 'electron' and use `process.resourcesPath` typed by electron's global augmentation (electron types are ambient when imported anywhere in project? The electron package declares `namespace NodeJS { interface Process { resourcesPath: string } }` in its own .d.ts, included because we import electron in other files? TypeScript includes all files in program, including electron.d.ts via imports; global augmentations apply project-wide). Likely fine; typecheck will tell.

