```ts
return JSON.stringify(...) ?? String(value)
```
If JSON.stringify throws, it jumps to catch and returns `[unloggable value]`, never trying String. That's a regression for BigInt logging (not used). The review asks formatting failures within the boundary; okay. Could improve:
```ts
try { const json = ...; return json ?? String(value); } catch { try { return redact(String(value)); } catch { return '[unloggable value]'; } }
```
This preserves BigInt as `String(1n)` = '1'. Is that worth it? It makes the fallback more useful and still safe (redacted). Do it for quality. Also for hostile with throwing toJSON but working toString, it would return the string; our test hostile throws both, falls back. Let's refine:
```ts
function describe(value) {
  if (typeof value === 'string') return redact(value);
  if (value instanceof Error) return redact(...);
  try {
    const json = JSON.stringify(value, replacer);
    if (json !== undefined) return json;
  } catch {
    // fall through to String
  }
  try { return redact(String(value)); } catch { return '[unloggable value]'; }
}
```
This is better. Update and rerun focused log test.

